# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SpotLens environment variables
# Copy to `.env` (Prisma reads .env; Next.js reads .env and .env.local).
#
# Nothing here is required to see a chart: with no .env at all, the curated
# market list and live candles still work. DATABASE_URL is what unlocks
# accounts, setups, the scanner, the journal and research; AUTH_SECRET is what
# lets you sign in. Everything else is optional and says so.
#
# Validated at boot by lib/env.ts, which warns in development and refuses to
# start in production. A blank value means "not configured", not "invalid".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Postgres (Neon in staging/prod, docker-compose locally).
# The chart works without a database — the curated asset list is read from
# lib/market-data/curated-assets.ts and candles come straight from the provider.
# Accounts, watchlist, settings and analysis history need it.
#
# On Neon, copy BOTH connection strings from the Connect dialog:
#   DATABASE_URL — with "Connection pooling" ON  (the -pooler host)
#   DIRECT_URL   — with "Connection pooling" OFF (used only by migrations,
#                  because Prisma's advisory locks need a direct connection)
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://spotlens:spotlens@localhost:5432/spotlens?schema=public"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://spotlens:spotlens@localhost:5432/spotlens?schema=public"

# Shadow database — a scratch database Prisma DROPS AND RECREATES.
#
# NEVER set this to DATABASE_URL or DIRECT_URL. `prisma migrate dev` and
# `prisma migrate diff --from-migrations` replay the migration chain into this
# database after wiping it; pointing it at the database the application uses
# destroys every row in it. That is not a hypothetical — it is how this
# project's development database was lost, by passing DIRECT_URL to
# `--shadow-database-url`. The command did what it documents.
#
# Use the disposable local Postgres from docker-compose:
#
#   npm run db:shadow      # starts it and creates a clean spotlens_shadow
#
# prisma/schema.prisma names this as `shadowDatabaseUrl`, so Prisma actually
# uses it — without that line Prisma invents its own shadow on the DIRECT_URL
# server instead. Leaving this unset does NOT make Prisma safe: it silently
# falls back to that invented shadow, which is why the guard requires a value.
#
# `npm run prisma:migrate` refuses to run unless this is set, local and
# different from the application's database — AND unless DIRECT_URL is itself
# local, because `migrate dev` resets the main database when it detects drift
# ("All data will be lost"), which no shadow configuration prevents. See
# scripts/check-shadow-db.ts.
#
# So `migrate dev` is for local development only. To apply migrations to a
# real database — Neon included — use `npm run prisma:deploy`
# (`prisma migrate deploy`), which needs no shadow database at all, never
# resets anything, and works with this variable unset.
SHADOW_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://spotlens:spotlens@localhost:5432/spotlens_shadow"

# Binance public REST base. No API key needed — public market data only.
# SpotLens never requests or stores trading-scoped keys.
BINANCE_API_BASE_URL="https://api.binance.com"
NEXT_PUBLIC_BINANCE_WS_BASE_URL="wss://stream.binance.com:9443"

# Auth.js. Generate a secret with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# AUTH_SECRET is the v5 name; NEXTAUTH_SECRET is still read for compatibility.
NEXTAUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"
AUTH_SECRET=""
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=""

# SpotLens uses email/password only, which is why Auth.js runs JWT sessions
# rather than database sessions — its Credentials provider does not support the
# database strategy.

# Rate limiting. Optional: without these, limits are enforced in-process,
# which is correct for a single instance and useless across several.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=""
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=""

# Billing, OAuth, queued jobs, cron and error tracking have no variables here
# because they have no code here. Each was a deliberate decision against
# building it for a single-user local app rather than something unfinished —
# see "Decisions already made" in CLAUDE.md. Every variable in this file is
# read by something.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local scanner (`npm run scanner`)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The scanner is a separate local process. It wakes shortly after each candle
# closes rather than polling, analyses the curated markets with the same engine
# the app uses, and updates the setups it is already tracking.
#
# All optional — the defaults below are what it uses if these are unset.

# Markets analysed at once. Ninety analyses fired together would be a burst of
# requests at a public endpoint; four keeps it to a trickle.
SCANNER_MAX_CONCURRENCY="4"

# Entry timeframes to scan, comma separated. Each is read against its own
# higher timeframe automatically.
SCANNER_TIMEFRAMES="H1,H4"

# How long to wait after a candle closes before analysing it. The exchange
# needs a moment to settle the final candle; asking at the instant of close
# reliably returns one that is still being written.
#
# This applies to `npm run scanner:once` as well as to the schedule. A manual
# pass waits only when it lands inside this window — rare, since a candle closes
# once an hour — and otherwise starts immediately. Set it to 0 to scan without
# waiting at all.
SCANNER_CLOSE_DELAY_MS="90000"

# Which account the scanner tracks setups against.
#
# Not needed while there is one account: the scanner uses it. Once a second
# account exists the scanner REFUSES TO RUN until this names one, because the
# owner decides who every setup, lifecycle event and notification belongs to —
# Telegram included. It used to pick the oldest account silently, which sent
# notifications to a chat nobody was watching.
SCANNER_USER_EMAIL=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Telegram notifications (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Create a bot with @BotFather and paste its token here. Server-side only: it
# is never sent to the browser, never returned by an API, never written to the
# database, and never logged. Leave blank to use in-app notifications only.
#
# Connect a chat from Settings → Notifications → Connect Telegram, which issues
# a one-time code you send to the bot. There is no Chat ID to copy by hand.
#
# The bot's @username is read from Telegram at runtime, and the chat id is
# established by the connection flow and stored against the account — neither
# belongs in here.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Confirmation alerts — a SECOND, separate Telegram bot (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase Q. Confirmation evidence appears candle by candle at every tracked
# level, which is far more traffic than the lifecycle events above. Sending both
# through one bot buries the messages that say a setup's situation actually
# changed, so confirmation has its own bot and the main one no longer carries
# any of it.
#
# This must be a genuinely different bot, not the same token twice: Telegram
# will not let a bot message someone who has never pressed Start on it, so the
# two bindings are separate and each needs its own connection.
#
# Create it yourself with @BotFather:
#   1. Open Telegram and message @BotFather
#   2. /newbot, then pick a display name and a username ending in "bot"
#   3. BotFather replies with a token — paste it here, between the quotes
#   4. Restart the app and the scanner, then connect it from
#      Settings → Notifications → Confirmation alerts
#
# Never paste this token into a chat, a source file, or an issue. Server-side
# only: like the token above it is never sent to the browser, never returned by
# an API, never written to the database, and stripped out of every error
# message before it can reach a row or a log.
#
# Leave blank and confirmation alerts are simply recorded in-app instead. The
# scanner does not fail, and the main bot is unaffected.
TELEGRAM_CONFIRMATION_BOT_TOKEN=""


# --- Coach -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. Without a key the Coach still works: it answers with SpotLens's own
# deterministic reading of the analysis. With one, that reading is replaced by a
# ChatGPT review of the same facts — the numbers are SpotLens's either way, and
# the model is never asked for one.
#
# Server-side only. It is read in lib/coach/config.ts, travels into a request
# header, and never reaches the browser, the database or a log.
OPENAI_API_KEY=""
# Optional. Defaults to gpt-5.6-terra. gpt-5.6-luna is cheaper, gpt-6-astra more
# capable. One request per explicit "Ask Coach" click; never from the scanner.
OPENAI_COACH_MODEL=""
